slug: exercise-stop-signs-and-ward-mobilisation
title: When to Stop — Exercise Stop Signs and Early Mobilisation on the Ward and ICU
area: Skills · Vitals & Monitoring
level: Clinical
best: 3rd year onwards
minutes: 12
hook: Symptoms beat numbers, and the hospital bed has its own rules. Learn the stop signs that end any session and the traffic-light criteria many units use before standing an unwell patient. || Symptoms numbers se upar hain, aur hospital bed ke apne qaide hain. Woh stop signs seekhein jo har session rok dete hain aur traffic-light criteria jo bohat si units bimar patient ko khara karne se pehle use karti hain.
goal: By the end, you'll list the stop-exercise signs, use a traffic-light check before mobilising, recognise deterioration with a track-and-trigger score and know when to hand over. || End tak aap stop-exercise signs ginwayenge, mobilise karne se pehle traffic-light check use karenge, track-and-trigger score se bigarti halat pehchanenge aur jaanenge ke kab hand over karna hai.

say: **Stop exercise immediately** for: chest pain or pressure (angina) or new palpitations; dizziness, confusion, unsteadiness, pallor or cyanosis; severe breathlessness or wheeze out of proportion to the effort; a **systolic blood pressure that falls with increasing work** or an SpO₂ below about **88–90%**; and **when the patient asks to stop**. On the ward and in ICU many units use traffic-light criteria before mobilising, with typical safe ranges of **heart rate about 40–130, mean arterial pressure 60–110 mmHg, SpO₂ ≥ 88–90%, respiratory rate below about 30–35** — always follow your local protocol and the medical team. || **Foran exercise rok dein** agar: seene ka dard ya dabao (angina) ya nayi dhadkan; chakkar, confusion, unsteadiness, pallor ya cyanosis; kaam ke mutabiq na hone wali shadeed saans ki takleef ya wheeze; **barhte kaam ke saath girta systolic blood pressure** ya SpO₂ takriban **88–90 %** se neeche; aur **jab patient khud rokne ko kahe**. Ward aur ICU mein bohat si units mobilise karne se pehle traffic-light criteria use karti hain, jin ki aam mehfooz ranges **heart rate takriban 40–130, mean arterial pressure 60–110 mmHg, SpO₂ ≥ 88–90 %, respiratory rate takriban 30–35 se kam** hain — hamesha apne local protocol aur medical team ki hidayat follow karein.
analogy: A traffic light for mobilisation works like a road junction. Green: go, with care. Amber: slow down, get advice or modify (shorter, assisted, sitting only). Red: stop, do not proceed until the medical team says so. The point is not to scare you out of mobilising; it is to give everyone the same answer before you start. || Mobilisation ki traffic light sarak ke chauraahay jaisi hai. Hari: chalo, ehtiyat se. Peeli: aahista, mashwara lein ya tabdeeli karein (chhota, madad ke saath, sirf baithna). Surkh: ruk jayein, medical team ke kehne tak aage na barhein. Maqsad aap ko mobilise karne se darana nahi; shuru karne se pehle sab ko ek jaisa jawab dena hai.
demo: vital-signs/concept:stop-signs :: The stop signs at a glance :: Watch the monitor read-outs as the effort rises. Notice the symptom chips and what changes: falling oxygen, rising breathlessness, a fall in systolic pressure. The rule: if a stop sign appears, the session ends and you check the patient, not the plan. || Effort barhne par monitor ki read-outs dekhein. Symptom chips aur jo cheez badalti hai dekhein: oxygen ka girna, saans ki takleef ka barhna, systolic pressure ka girna. Qaida: stop sign aaye to session khatam aur aap patient ko dekhte hain, plan ko nahi.
check: During a treadmill session a patient's systolic pressure drops from 150 to 120 as the speed increases, and they feel light-headed. What do you do? || Treadmill session mein speed barhne par patient ka systolic pressure 150 se 120 par aa jata hai aur sar halka mehsoos hota hai. Kya karein?
ok: Stop, sit the patient down, check pulse, BP and symptoms, and inform the medical team || Rok dein, patient ko bitha dein, pulse, BP aur symptoms check karein aur medical team ko batayein || Yes — a systolic fall with increasing workload is a stop sign (possible heart failure, arrhythmia or outflow obstruction). || Haan — barhte workload ke saath systolic ka girna stop sign hai (mumkina heart failure, arrhythmia ya outflow obstruction).
no: Slow down slightly and carry on || Zara aahista kar ke jari rakhein || A fall in blood pressure with effort is not a reason to carry on. || Effort ke saath blood pressure ka girna jari rakhne ki wajah nahi.
no: Encourage the patient to push through || Patient ko hausla dein ke aage barhe || Pushing through a stop sign is unsafe. || Stop sign ke bawajood aage barhna mehfooz nahi.
compare: A traffic-light check before mobilising || Mobilise karne se pehle traffic-light check
head: System || System ;; Green (go) || Hari (chalo) ;; Red (stop, ask first) || Surkh (ruko, pehle poochein)
row: Heart rate || Heart rate ;; About 40–130 bpm || Takriban 40–130 bpm ;; New arrhythmia, resting rate over about 130 or under 40, or new chest pain || Nayi arrhythmia, aaraam ka rate takriban 130 se upar ya 40 se neeche, ya naya seene ka dard
row: Blood pressure || Blood pressure ;; MAP about 60–110 mmHg; systolic about 90–180 || MAP takriban 60–110 mmHg; systolic takriban 90–180 ;; Systolic below 90 or over about 200, or a new large fall on sitting up || Systolic 90 se neeche ya takriban 200 se upar, ya baithne par nayi bari girawat
row: Oxygen and breathing || Oxygen aur saans ;; SpO₂ at or above 88–90 % (or the target); respiratory rate under about 30 || SpO₂ 88–90 % (ya target) ya upar; respiratory rate takriban 30 se kam ;; SpO₂ below target, respiratory rate over about 35, or new severe breathlessness || SpO₂ target se neeche, respiratory rate takriban 35 se upar, ya nayi shadeed saans ki takleef
row: Other || Doosre ;; Alert and cooperative; temperature normal; pain controlled; lines and drains secured || Hoshyar aur taawun karne wala; temperature normal; dard qabu mein; lines aur drains mehfooz ;; Fever, new confusion, uncontrolled pain, unstable fracture or an unsecured airway or line || Bukhar, nayi confusion, be-qabu dard, unstable fracture ya ghair-mehfooz airway ya line
mistake: **Applying one fixed number to everyone, or ignoring the trend.** A resting heart rate of 120 in a patient with a normal trend of 70 is a very different problem from 120 in someone who always runs fast with atrial fibrillation. Use the **trend and the context** (diagnosis, medicines, baseline) as well as the number. Equally, do not skip the chart review: check the latest observations, the doctor's activity orders and the lines, drains and infusions before you move anyone. || **Sab par ek hi number lagana ya trend ko nazar-andaz karna.** Aaraam ka heart rate 120 us patient mein jis ka normal trend 70 ho, us se bilkul mukhtalif masla hai jo hamesha tez atrial fibrillation mein rehta ho. Number ke saath **trend aur context** (tashkhees, dawaiyan, baseline) bhi use karein. Isi tarah chart review chhorein nahi: kisi ko hilane se pehle taaza observations, doctor ke activity orders aur lines, drains aur infusions check karein.
check: Why use a track-and-trigger score such as NEWS2 on the ward? || Ward par NEWS2 jaisa track-and-trigger score kyun use karte hain?
ok: It combines respiratory rate, SpO₂, blood pressure, pulse, temperature and alertness into one score that flags deterioration early || Yeh respiratory rate, SpO₂, blood pressure, pulse, temperature aur hoshyari ko ek score mein jorta hai jo bigarti halat ko jaldi flag karta hai || Yes — a rising score tells you to escalate before the patient crashes. || Haan — barhta score batata hai ke patient ke girne se pehle escalate karein.
no: To replace clinical judgement || Clinical judgement ki jagah lene ke liye || It supports judgement; it does not replace it. || Yeh judgement ki madad karta hai; usay badalta nahi.
no: To decide the exercise prescription || Exercise prescription tay karne ke liye || It flags deterioration, not exercise dose. || Yeh bigarti halat flag karta hai, exercise dose nahi.
clinic: **Mobilise early, in stages, and with a plan.** Early mobilisation in ICU and on the ward reduces complications and shortens stay when done safely. Stage it (bed exercises → sitting on the edge → standing → marching on the spot → walking), monitor heart rate, SpO₂, blood pressure and symptoms at each stage, and have the team ready. Document the stage reached, the observations, the assistance needed and the reason for any stop, so the next shift continues from there. || **Jaldi, marhala-war aur plan ke saath mobilise karein.** ICU aur ward mein early mobilisation mehfooz tareeqay se ki jaye to complications kam aur qiyam chhota karti hai. Isay marhalon mein karein (bistar ki exercises → kinare par baithna → khara hona → jagah par march → chalna), har marhale par heart rate, SpO₂, blood pressure aur symptoms monitor karein, aur team tayyar rakhein. Pohnchne wala marhala, observations, darkaar madad aur kisi bhi stop ki wajah likhein taake agli shift wahin se jari rakhe.
evidence: Early mobilisation in critically ill adults is **safe and feasible** (adverse events occur in fewer than 1 in 100 sessions in large studies) and is associated with better physical function at discharge, though large trials show that very early, high-dose protocols are not always better than usual care. The practical message is to mobilise as early as it is safe, in a stepwise, monitored way, rather than to chase a fixed schedule. || Critically ill adults mein early mobilisation **mehfooz aur mumkin** hai (bari studies mein 100 mein se 1 se kam sessions mein adverse events) aur discharge par behtar jismani function se jori hai, halankeh bare trials dikhate hain ke bohat jaldi, zyada dose wale protocols hamesha usual care se behtar nahi. Amali paigham yeh hai ke jitni jaldi mehfooz ho utni jaldi, marhala-war, monitor kar ke mobilise karein, kisi fixed schedule ke peechay na bhagein.
reveal: A patient's SpO₂ drops to 86% when you stand them, but recovers to 92% within a minute of sitting. Is that a failure? || Patient ko khara karne par SpO₂ 86 % par aa jata hai lekin baithne ke ek minute ke andar 92 % ho jata hai. Kya yeh nakami hai? || Not necessarily. It is an informative result: the patient tolerates the activity poorly, so you stop, rest, check symptoms and report it. You can try again with a shorter bout, pacing, supplemental oxygen if prescribed or breathing control. Document it so the team can adjust. The failure is to continue through the stop sign or to record nothing. || Zaroori nahi. Yeh maloomati nateeja hai: patient activity ko kam bardasht karta hai, is liye aap rukte hain, rest, symptoms check aur report karte hain. Aap chhote bout, pacing, prescribe ho to supplemental oxygen ya breathing control ke saath dobara koshish kar sakte hain. Document karein taake team tabdeeli kar sake. Nakami stop sign ke bawajood jari rakhna ya kuch na likhna hai.
check: Which of these is a **red** (do not mobilise until reviewed) finding? || In mein se kaun si **surkh** finding hai (review tak mobilise na karein)?
ok: New chest pain with a resting heart rate over 130 || Naya seene ka dard aur aaraam ka heart rate 130 se upar || Yes — new chest pain with a fast rate needs medical review before activity. || Haan — naya seene ka dard tez rate ke saath activity se pehle medical review chahta hai.
no: SpO₂ 95 % and a heart rate of 80 || SpO₂ 95 % aur heart rate 80 || That is within the green range. || Yeh hari range ke andar hai.
no: Mild soreness after yesterday's walk || Kal ki walk ke baad halka dard || Mild muscle soreness is not a stop sign. || Muscles ka halka dard stop sign nahi.
safety: **Call the medical team or emergency help** for chest pain with sweating or breathlessness, a systolic pressure below 90 with symptoms, new confusion, loss of consciousness, a sustained arrhythmia, severe breathlessness or cyanosis. For cardiac arrest call for help, start chest compressions and use an AED if available. Know the emergency number and the location of the equipment before you start working on any ward. || **Medical team ya emergency madad bulayein** paseenay ya saans ki takleef ke saath seene ka dard, symptoms ke saath systolic pressure 90 se neeche, nayi confusion, behoshi, musalsal arrhythmia, shadeed saans ki takleef ya cyanosis par. Cardiac arrest mein madad bulayein, chest compressions shuru karein aur AED ho to use karein. Kisi bhi ward mein kaam shuru karne se pehle emergency number aur equipment ki jagah jaan lein.
summary: Stop for chest pain, dizziness, pallor, severe breathlessness, falling systolic pressure, SpO₂ below about 88–90 % or when the patient asks. || Seene ka dard, chakkar, pallor, shadeed saans ki takleef, girta systolic pressure, SpO₂ takriban 88–90 % se neeche ya patient ke kehne par ruk jayein.
summary: Before mobilising a ward or ICU patient use traffic-light criteria, the trend, the notes and the team's orders, and use NEWS2 to spot deterioration. || Ward ya ICU patient ko mobilise karne se pehle traffic-light criteria, trend, notes aur team ke orders use karein, aur bigarti halat pehchanne ke liye NEWS2 use karein.
summary: Mobilise early, in stages and documented; a stop is useful information, so record it and hand over. || Jaldi, marhala-war aur document kar ke mobilise karein; stop kaam ki maloomat hai, isay likhein aur hand over karein.
card: List four stop signs for exercise. || Exercise ke chaar stop signs ginwayein. || Chest pain, dizziness or pallor, severe breathlessness, falling systolic pressure or SpO₂ below about 88–90 %, or the patient asks to stop. || Seene ka dard, chakkar ya pallor, shadeed saans ki takleef, girta systolic pressure ya SpO₂ takriban 88–90 % se neeche, ya patient rokne ko kahe.
card: Typical safe ranges for mobilising on ICU? || ICU mein mobilise karne ki aam mehfooz ranges? || Heart rate about 40–130, MAP 60–110, SpO₂ ≥ 88–90 %, respiratory rate under about 30–35 (follow local protocol). || Heart rate takriban 40–130, MAP 60–110, SpO₂ ≥ 88–90 %, respiratory rate takriban 30–35 se kam (local protocol follow karein).
card: What does NEWS2 combine? || NEWS2 kya kya milata hai? || Respiratory rate, SpO₂, systolic BP, pulse, temperature and level of consciousness into one early-warning score. || Respiratory rate, SpO₂, systolic BP, pulse, temperature aur hoshyari ki satah ek early-warning score mein.
card: What should you record after a session that stopped early? || Jaldi ruk gayi session ke baad kya likhein? || The stage reached, the observations, the assistance needed and the reason for the stop. || Pohnchne wala marhala, observations, darkaar madad aur rukne ki wajah.
deeper: Vital signs skill in the Skills Lab || /skills/vital-signs || The stop signs and ward criteria in 3D. || Stop signs aur ward criteria 3D mein.
deeper: Red flags screening lesson || /learn/red-flags-screening || Recognise the patient who should not exercise today. || Us patient ko pehchanein jo aaj exercise na kare.
source: Royal College of Physicians. National Early Warning Score (NEWS) 2: Standardising the assessment of acute-illness severity in the NHS. London: RCP; 2017. || National Early Warning Score NEWS2
source: Hodgson CL, Stiller K, Needham DM, et al. Expert consensus and recommendations on safety criteria for active mobilization of mechanically ventilated critically ill adults. Crit Care. 2014;18:658. || safety criteria active mobilization mechanically ventilated critically ill adults
